  "response": "I’ll scope this to the three requested modules, reconcile the scan findings that directly apply to them, and inspect the relevant code so the proposed types and names fit the actual control flow.\n\nThe scoped “petty agent-risk” cluster is eight findings across the three files. Several are duplicated because `crimes` attributes nested callback returns to their containing plugin functions.\n\n| Finding type | File | Symbols | Count |\n|---|---|---|---:|\n| `name_behavior_mismatch` | `src/index.ts` | `build`, `buildAll` | 2 |\n| `name_behavior_mismatch` | `src/rollup.ts` | `getRollupConfig`, `buildStart` | 2 |\n| `option_bag_junk_drawer` | `src/rollup.ts` | `getRollupConfig` | 1 |\n| `return_shape_roulette` | `src/esbuild/svelte.ts` | `sveltePlugin`, `setup`, anonymous load callback | 3 |\n\n## `name_behavior_mismatch`\n\n### What can go wrong today\n\n- `getRollupConfig` sounds like a read or pure calculation, but it calls `setSilent`, loads package data and dependencies, mutates the aliased `options.dts` object, and creates a cleanup plugin.\n- An agent could call it speculatively, cache its result, or reuse the same `NormalizedOptions`, unaware that configuration state may be mutated.\n- `buildAll` does much more than build: it terminates a prior success process, cleans outputs, runs every format, copies public files, and launches an `onSuccess` process.\n- `build` is a public imperative API whose name is conventional. Renaming it would create needless compatibility risk.\n- Rollup’s `buildStart` is a required lifecycle-hook name, so it cannot simply be renamed. The misleading behavior belongs in its body.\n\n### Refactor proposal\n\n- Preserve the exported `build(options)` API as a thin compatibility boundary.\n- Extract its per-configuration body into `runConfiguredBuild(...)`.\n- Rename and decompose internal functions:\n\n  - `mainTasks` → `runJavaScriptBuildPipeline`\n  - `buildAll` → `runBuildCycle`\n  - `doOnSuccessCleanup` → `stopPreviousOnSuccessTask`\n  - extract `cleanBuildOutputs`\n  - extract `buildAllFormats`\n  - extract `copyPublicAssets`\n  - extract `startOnSuccessTask`\n\n- Split `getRollupConfig` into:\n\n  - `loadDtsRollupContext(cwd)` for package/dependency reads\n  - `createRollupConfig(request, context)` for config construction\n  - `createDeclarationCleanupPlugin(...)` for the deferred cleanup behavior\n\n- Move `setSilent(options.silent)` to `startRollup`, where the runtime side effect is explicit.\n- Keep the required `buildStart` hook, but make it a one-line delegate:\n\n```ts\nasync buildStart() {\n  await cleanDeclarationOutputs(cleanupOptions)\n}\n```\n\nThis makes `createRollupConfig` effectively pure while retaining Rollup’s mandated hook name.\n\n## `option_bag_junk_drawer`\n\n### Finding\n\n`src/rollup.ts:getRollupConfig` reads at least ten unrelated properties from the broad `NormalizedOptions` object. The parameter is typed, so “implicit shape” slightly overstates the issue, but the function still depends on too much of the global build configuration.\n\n### What can go wrong today\n\nAn agent adding a declaration-build option may:\n\n- Read another top-level field instead of deciding which Rollup concern owns it.\n- Accidentally couple input resolution, output naming, cleanup, and logging.\n- Mutate `options.dts`, believing `const dtsOptions = options.dts || {}` created an independent object.\n- Construct tests with incomplete `NormalizedOptions` fixtures that happen to pass through casts.\n\n### Refactor proposal\n\nMap `NormalizedOptions` once at the orchestration boundary into a declaration-specific request:\n\n```ts\ninterface DtsRollupRequest {\n  input: DtsInputOptions\n  output: DtsOutputOptions\n  lifecycle: DtsLifecycleOptions\n}\n\ninterface DtsInputOptions {\n  entry: NormalizedOptions[\"entry\"]\n  tsconfig: NormalizedOptions[\"tsconfig\"]\n  compilerOptions: NonNullable<NormalizedOptions[\"dts\"]>[\"compilerOptions\"]\n  resolve: NonNullable<NormalizedOptions[\"dts\"]>[\"resolve\"]\n  external: NormalizedOptions[\"external\"]\n}\n\ninterface DtsOutputOptions {\n  formats: NormalizedOptions[\"format\"]\n  outDir: NormalizedOptions[\"outDir\"]\n  outExtension: NormalizedOptions[\"outExtension\"]\n  banner: NonNullable<NormalizedOptions[\"dts\"]>[\"banner\"]\n  footer: NonNullable<NormalizedOptions[\"dts\"]>[\"footer\"]\n  cjsInterop: NormalizedOptions[\"cjsInterop\"]\n}\n\ninterface DtsLifecycleOptions {\n  shouldClean: boolean\n}\n```\n\nUse indexed access types so these narrower records do not become a second authority for option types.\n\nThen extract:\n\n- `resolveDtsEntry(input)`\n- `createTsResolveOptions(input)`\n- `createRollupInputOptions(input, context)`\n- `createRollupOutputOptions(output, context)`\n- `createDeclarationCleanupPlugin(lifecycle, outDir)`\n\nThe adapter should clone nested DTS options before normalization, eliminating mutation of the caller’s `NormalizedOptions`.\n\n## `return_shape_roulette`\n\n### Findings\n\nAll three findings in `src/esbuild/svelte.ts` originate from nested callbacks:\n\n- `sveltePlugin` itself consistently returns an esbuild `Plugin`.\n- `setup` returns `void`, as required by esbuild.\n- The resolver, loader, diagnostic conversion, and Svelte preprocess callbacks return different object shapes appropriate to different external APIs.\n\nThe detector is therefore identifying a real readability problem but attributing it too broadly.\n\n### What can go wrong today\n\nAn agent could:\n\n- Confuse an esbuild resolve result with an esbuild load result.\n- Add `{ contents }`, `{ errors }`, or `{ warnings }` to the wrong callback.\n- Assume `convertMessage` always returns a location.\n- Return a Svelte preprocessor result where an esbuild `OnLoadResult` is expected.\n- Extend the large anonymous loader without realizing which branches represent successful compilation versus dependency or compilation failure.\n- Continue using `any` for Svelte diagnostics, losing checks around `start`, `end`, and line indexing.\n\n### Refactor proposal\n\nExtract and explicitly type every callback boundary:\n\n```ts\nfunction resolveSvelteCss(args: OnResolveArgs): OnResolveResult\n\nasync function loadSvelteModule(\n  args: OnLoadArgs,\n  context: SveltePluginContext,\n): Promise<OnLoadResult>\n\nfunction toEsbuildMessage(\n  diagnostic: SvelteDiagnosticLike,\n  source: SourceContext,\n): PartialMessage\n```\n\nAlso extract:\n\n- `loadSvelteCompiler(): SvelteCompiler`\n- `preprocessSvelteSource(...)`\n- `compileSvelteSource(...)`\n- `recordExtractedCss(...)`\n\nFor the internal compilation step, use a discriminated result:\n\n```ts\ntype SvelteCompileOutcome =\n  | { kind: \"compiled\"; contents: string; warnings: PartialMessage[] }\n  | { kind: \"failed\"; errors: PartialMessage[] }\n```\n\nOnly `loadSvelteModule` should translate that internal outcome into esbuild’s `OnLoadResult`. This centralizes the legitimate multiple shapes at the external API adapter.\n\nThe inline TypeScript preprocessor should also receive an explicit Svelte preprocessor return type, making `{ code }` and `{ code, map }` intentional variants rather than inferred object literals.\n\n## Recommended sequence\n\n1. Refactor `src/esbuild/svelte.ts` first. It has existing test coverage and the changes can be mostly extraction plus type annotations.\n2. Refactor `src/rollup.ts`, first preventing mutation of `options.dts`, then separating context loading, pure config construction, and lifecycle plugins.\n3. Refactor `src/index.ts` last because it has the widest fan-out and the largest nested orchestration surface. Keep `build` API-compatible and extract one responsibility at a time.\n4. Add focused tests for:\n\n   - Svelte resolve, successful load, warning conversion, and error conversion.\n   - Rollup input/output config construction without filesystem or global logger mutation.\n   - Build-cycle ordering: cleanup → format builds → public copy → success hook.\n   - Rebuild failure restoring the previous dependency set.\n\n5. Run typecheck and tests after each file, then rescan. The expected result is removal of the eight scoped findings through structural clarity—not suppression. The adjacent `large_function` findings in all three modules should also fall substantially as a consequence.",
